5. Dataset Description: \cf0 Kelp forests are among the most productive marine ecosystems, yet they are facing losses from climate change and localized surges in sea urchin populations. Unchecked urchin grazing can turn kelp forests into barrens, where sea urchins prevent kelp recovery and maintain the barren state. In such cases, kelp restoration requires targeted efforts that account for herbivory and its effects on kelp establishment. Here, we identified the extent to which urchin and red turban snail herbivory limits recovery and tested whether reducing grazing pressure could facilitate kelp recruitment in a setting where restoration has been proposed yet large-scale grazer removals are not yet permitted: Barkley Sound, British Columbia, Canada (48.82\'baN\'9648.88\'baN and 125.07\'baW\'96125.21\'baW). We used grazer exclusion devices and monitored the survival and growth of outplanted giant kelp(
\f1\i Macrocystis pyrifera
\f0\i0 )
\f1\i  
\f0\i0 on small (2 cm\super  \nosupersub diameter) and large (6 cm diameter) green gravel at six restoration sites that naturally varied in grazing pressure between March and August 2024. Lower site-level urchin and red turban snail densities improved kelp survival and growth. Exclusion reduced grazer density within plots, yet kelp survival and growth remained low, suggesting that cage design, green gravel, or environmental conditions also limited kelp establishment. Larger gravel improved kelp survival compared to small gravel. These results suggest that grazing limits early kelp establishment at some sites, which cannot be mitigated by small-scale exclusion or active kelp outplanting. Rather, this experiment provides evidence to guide management decisions and inform the development of broader-scale grazer mitigation strategies.
\f2 \

\f0 \cf4 \
