METHODOLOGICAL INFORMATION\
--------------------------\
\
1. Details on methodology of data collection can be found in the associated publication:\
\
\cf4 Rehill T, Dykman LN, Schuster JM, Baum JK (2026) \cf2 \cb3 Grazing pressure limits giant kelp (Macrocystis pyrifera) recruitment and restoration potential in a temperate fjord\cf4 \cb1 . Marine Biology. \cf6 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 https://doi.org/10.1111/jpy.70232.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 General conventions used in all files:\
- Missing values are recorded as NA (blank cells in raw files).\
- Sites: Ed King, Dixon, Ellis N, Ellis S, Nanat, Taylor. Site level data are given as one row per site per month.\
- Months: March (deployment), April, May, June, August 2024. Surveys at Ed King and Taylor did not begin until April.\
- Grazers: red urchin (Mesocentrotus franciscanus; "ru"), green urchin (Strongylocentrotus droebachiensis; "gu"), purple urchin (Strongylocentrotus purpuratus; "pu"), red turban snail (Pomaulax gibberosus; "sn"/"ts"), and northern abalone (Haliotis kamtschatkana; "ab"). Other invertebrates recorded: "pyc" (Pycnopodia helianthoides) and "cuc" (sea cucumbers).\
- Treatments: treatment = caged vs. open plots; gravel_size = small (2 cm) vs. large (6 cm) green gravel.\
- Densities are individuals per m2. Plot-level densities are standardized by surface area, because cage walls were included in counts (caged plot area = 4.5 m2; open plot area = 1 m2). Site-level densities are counts divided by the number of 1 m2 quadrats surveyed.\cf6 \
